    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "High-temperature alteration of siliceous mudstone to clinker is accompanied by the formation of blunt- tipped opening mode fractures. Blunt-tipped fractures result from the growth of initially circular pores inherited from the diatomaceous protolith and their coalescence due to thinning and rupture of bridges between pores. Circular pores coalesce to elongate pores and blunt-tipped fractures that propagate in a zig-zag path characteristic of ductile fracture in metals. Less pronounced pore growth and coalescence along the sides of fractures leads to an concurrent increase in fracture aperture. This process of fracture formation by void growth and coalescence is inferred to result from diffusive mass transfer and possible bulk melt movement during partial melting of clinker. This process differs from fracture in ductile metals by crystal plastic flow in response to mechanical loading. The preferred elongation of coalescing pores and local rupture of pore bridges is explained by a tensile sintering stress resulting from the thermodynamic tendency of the system for energy minimization of solid and liquid surfaces. Unlike ductile fracture in metals where void growth is confined to a plastic zone close to the fracture tip, minimization of surface energy in clinker also allows void growth away from the stress concentration of existing fractures. It is suggested that similar fracture processes are significant in metamorphic and magmatic systems as well as in reactive diagenetic environments.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
